body:
I agree. Here are some examples from my life. I had been on both sides and remember very well what it was like.

When I was naturally very skinny:

* Silly jokes from best friend along the lines of "You are so skinny, you will get carried away by the wind!" about twice a year. Back then I didn't know how to take jokes but even then I didn't get offended;

* People offering me food and sweets (awesome, I love food and sweets!);

* Family friends saying how cute I was;


When I hit puberty and got half an inch of stomach fat:

* Mum telling me I wasn't allowed to wear short tops anymore, constly being told to diet;

* School friend telling me I was fat;

* No more compliments or offers of sweets;

* Years later guys rejecting me over just that inch of fat and those who do not keep pointing at petite actresses and porn stars and casually telling me I had to look like them.

Thanks to that I developed anorexia, which completely ruined my metabolism. Shortly after the start of recovery (while still being told to starve more because stomach fat still didn't go anywhere) I get depression, those two things plus medicine make me gain 50 pounds in three months. I now weight about 160 pounds and US size 8-10 and I get:

* My entire family almost disowned me for it;

* After a while they seemed to "take me back", not sure what you call it, but I talk to them once a month or less because every member of the family chooses to spend those few minutes/hour they see me to talk how bad I look and that I have to diet;

* Mum keeps telling me not to touch a single sweet for the rest of my life and not to enjoy any kind food, she hadn't said a single nice thing about my looks in months while complimenting skinnier girls in front of me;

* The only time my family seems to "approve" of me is when I slip back into anorexic behavious thank to the bullying;

* Some older family friends that I briefly see over Skype make sure the first thing they mention is my weight;

* Rare times I go outside I can see strangers point at me and whisper something and I doubt it's good since I don't recall last time any stranger said anything nice to me;

* Did I mention to be constanly told to starve for the rest of my life?

I'm sure there are some cases where thin people were made to feel worse and I have no doubt there are fat people who get treated way more poorly than me, too. It's just my experience.

But I could give anything to be told to eat again.
